In larger nets (add LayerNorm blocks / a second head) the same first panic is followed by the `Should have handle for tensor` / `ordering.rs` cascade before the `CallError` surfaces, and each intermediate panic is visible only as a `log::warn!`.

**Expected behavior**

The loop prints five finite, decreasing losses and `completed without corruption`. When a cached autotune winner fails launch validation, the tuner should fall back to another candidate (or re-tune) rather than panic; and a failed op on the device-runner thread should fail the stream loudly rather than leave registered-but-never-written tensor handles behind.

**Additional context**

*The failing problem* (instrumented the validator to print it): the forward head matmul `[512,128] @ [128,7]`, rhs strides `[7,1]` → canonical stride 28 bytes, under the 16-byte async-copy threshold checked by `validate_async_copy_with_problem` (`cubek-matmul/src/components/global/read/strategy/base.rs`).

*Defect 1 — the autotune key overstates stride alignment.* `MatmulAutotuneKey` computes `lhs_stride_factor` / `rhs_stride_factor` from the **anchored** dims, not the real strides (`cubek-matmul/src/strategy/tune_key.rs`: `stride_factor(k_anchored, …)`). `anchor(7) = 8` keys this problem as 32-byte aligned; `anchor(126) = 128` keys a 504-byte-stride problem as 512-byte aligned. Problems that fail the async-copy/TMA validators therefore share buckets with problems that pass them, and a winner tuned on an aligned member is an invalid config for the others — validated only *after* autotune has committed. (Our real workload's autotune cache shows these collision buckets directly: the `Linear(126, _)` forward/backward problems share buckets with the 128-wide hidden-layer ones.)

*Defect 2 — nothing gates these strategies on alignment, so an invalid config can win autotune.* `Strategy::SpecializedCyclicCmma` / `SpecializedCyclicMma` read through `AsyncPartialCyclicLoading`, but they are registered in the `accelerated` group of `burn-cubecl/src/kernel/matmul/tune/base.rs` with no alignment gate — while the `tma` group applies exactly the `stride_factor >= 4` threshold these need. An under-aligned problem therefore hands them `PRIORITY_MAX`, and the failure only appears at setup, after autotune has committed to the winner, where `.expect("Should run when selected by autotune.")` in `cubecl-runtime/src/tune/local.rs` turns it into a panic. The fix belongs in selection, not in recovery — see the comment below for the full root cause.

*Defect 3 — the panic is swallowed and the stream keeps going.* Fire-and-forget tasks on the device-runner thread run under `catch_unwind` and only `log::warn!` the payload (`cubecl-common/src/device/handle/channel.rs`). Outputs are registered before execution, so a panicked op leaves live, never-written tensor handles behind — this is the silent-NaN mechanism, and it is unchanged in pre.2 (only blocking calls propagate errors, via the oneshot `CallError` path). A failed task should poison the stream/queue loudly.

PRs: defect 1 (key reports real stride alignment) — https://github.com/tracel-ai/cubek/pull/488; defect 2 (gate the async-copy strategies on that alignment) — see the comment below. They are only meaningful together: the gate reads the stride factors off the key, so it cannot fire until the key stops deriving them from the anchored dims.

An earlier attempt at defect 2 made the tuner fall back to the remaining candidates on a launch failure (cubecl#1510). That was rejected upstream, correctly: it would have to hand the real inputs to a second candidate, and autotune must never clone them or in-place operations write into the copy. Preventing the selection is the right layer.

One more observation from validating those PRs against burn main's pinned revs (cubecl `f0721ea`, cubek `611491b`): on sm_89 this repro no longer crashes there — but only because the offending kernel now fails *compilation* at tune time with `UnsupportedOp("plane.elect")`, surfacing as another swallowed device-thread panic (defect 3), so it never becomes the cached winner. The repro's crash should be verified against the published `0.22.0-pre.2` crates as specified above; the defective `.expect`/swallow paths are unchanged on main.

Environment:

- burn 0.22.0-pre.2 (cubek-matmul 0.3.0-pre.2, cubecl 0.11.0-pre.2); pre.1 exhibits the silent-NaN variant
- RTX 4080 SUPER (sm_89), CUDA 12.9, Linux
- f32 (not bf16-specific)

## Describe the bug

On the CUDA backend, `burn::backend::Cuda` (which is
`Fusion<CubeBackend<CudaRuntime, …>>`) produces a **numerically wrong** result for
the *batched* forward pass of a small MLP whose head narrows to a single output
column (`… -> Linear(_, 1) -> tanh`, output shape `[N, 1]`).

The **same model** with the **same weights**, run on the **same GPU** but with the
`Fusion<…>` wrapper removed (`CubeBackend<CudaRuntime, …>` directly), matches the
CPU `NdArray` oracle to f32 noise. So does the fused backend when the batch is fed
**one row at a time** (`[1, 1]` output). Only the *fused + batched + narrow-output*
combination is wrong.

The error is large (~1e-1 to ~1e0, on outputs bounded to `(-1, 1)` by `tanh`) and
**deterministic** run-to-run — a fusion correctness bug, not drift or
non-determinism. Its signature is distinctive: **row 0 is correct, every later row
is wrong.**

## To Reproduce

Self-contained cargo project (no external assets). Full sources below.

**`Cargo.toml`**

```toml
[package]
name = "burn-fusion-narrow-mlp"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2024"
publish = false

[dependencies]
burn = { version = "0.21.0", features = ["ndarray", "cuda"] }
# The un-fused CUDA backend: the exact inner backend `burn::backend::Cuda` wraps.
burn-cubecl = "0.21.0"
cubecl = { version = "0.10.0", features = ["cuda"] }
```

**`src/main.rs`**

```rust
use burn::backend::NdArray;
use burn::nn::{LeakyRelu, LeakyReluConfig, Linear, LinearConfig};
use burn::prelude::*;
use burn::record::{FullPrecisionSettings, NamedMpkFileRecorder};
use burn::tensor::activation::tanh;

type Cpu = NdArray;
/// CUDA with kernel fusion ON — this is what ships as `burn::backend::Cuda`.
type GpuFused = burn::backend::Cuda;
/// CUDA with kernel fusion OFF — the exact inner backend `Cuda` wraps in `Fusion`.
type GpuNoFusion = burn_cubecl::CubeBackend<cubecl::cuda::CudaRuntime, f32, i32, u8>;

const IN: usize = 36;
const HIDDEN: usize = 64;
const MID: usize = 9;
const N: usize = 5; // batch size (> 1 is required to trigger the bug)

/// `IN -> HIDDEN -> MID -> 1`, LeakyReLU between, `tanh` on the scalar output.
#[derive(Module, Debug)]
struct Mlp<B: Backend> {
    fc1: Linear<B>,
    fc2: Linear<B>,
    fc3: Linear<B>,
    act: LeakyRelu,
}

impl<B: Backend> Mlp<B> {
    fn new(device: &B::Device) -> Self {
        Mlp {
            fc1: LinearConfig::new(IN, HIDDEN).init(device),
            fc2: LinearConfig::new(HIDDEN, MID).init(device),
            fc3: LinearConfig::new(MID, 1).init(device),
            act: LeakyReluConfig::new().init(),
        }
    }
    fn forward(&self, x: Tensor<B, 2>) -> Tensor<B, 2> {
        let x = self.act.forward(self.fc1.forward(x));
        let x = self.act.forward(self.fc2.forward(x));
        tanh(self.fc3.forward(x)) // [N, 1]
    }
}

type Recorder = NamedMpkFileRecorder<FullPrecisionSettings>;

fn input_data() -> Vec<f32> {
    (0..N * IN).map(|i| (i as f32 * 0.1).sin()).collect()
}
fn encode<B: Backend>(device: &B::Device) -> Tensor<B, 2> {
    Tensor::<B, 1>::from_floats(input_data().as_slice(), device).reshape([N, IN])
}
fn to_vec<B: Backend>(t: Tensor<B, 2>) -> Vec<f32> {
    t.into_data().to_vec::<f32>().unwrap()
}
fn maxdiff(a: &[f32], b: &[f32]) -> f32 {
    a.iter().zip(b).map(|(x, y)| (x - y).abs()).fold(0.0f32, f32::max)
}

fn main() {
    let cpu_dev = Default::default();
    let fused_dev = Default::default();
    let nofus_dev = Default::default();

    // Build the net once on the CPU and persist it, so every backend loads the
    // *same* weights (fresh random init differs per backend/RNG otherwise).
    Cpu::seed(&cpu_dev, 1);
    let net_cpu = Mlp::<Cpu>::new(&cpu_dev);
    let stem = std::env::temp_dir().join(format!("burn_fusion_narrow_{}", std::process::id()));
    net_cpu.clone().save_file(&stem, &Recorder::new()).expect("save net");

    let net_cpu = net_cpu.load_file(&stem, &Recorder::new(), &cpu_dev).expect("reload cpu");
    let net_fused = Mlp::<GpuFused>::new(&fused_dev)
        .load_file(&stem, &Recorder::new(), &fused_dev).expect("load fused");
    let net_nofus = Mlp::<GpuNoFusion>::new(&nofus_dev)
        .load_file(&stem, &Recorder::new(), &nofus_dev).expect("load un-fused");

    let out_cpu = to_vec(net_cpu.forward(encode::<Cpu>(&cpu_dev)));
    let out_nofus = to_vec(net_nofus.forward(encode::<GpuNoFusion>(&nofus_dev)));
    let out_fused = to_vec(net_fused.forward(encode::<GpuFused>(&fused_dev)));

    // Fused CUDA, one row at a time (batch = 1, output shape [1, 1]).
    let full = encode::<GpuFused>(&fused_dev);
    let out_single: Vec<f32> = (0..N)
        .map(|r| to_vec(net_fused.forward(full.clone().slice([r..r + 1, 0..IN])))[0])
        .collect();

    std::fs::remove_file(stem.with_extension("mpk")).ok();

    println!("MLP {IN}->{HIDDEN}->{MID}->1 (+tanh), batch N={N}, output shape [N,1]\n");
    println!("  max|cpu - cuda_unfused_batched| = {:.3e}", maxdiff(&out_cpu, &out_nofus));
    println!("  max|cpu - cuda_fused_batched|   = {:.3e}   <-- BUG", maxdiff(&out_cpu, &out_fused));
    println!("  max|cpu - cuda_fused_per_row|   = {:.3e}", maxdiff(&out_cpu, &out_single));
    println!("\n  cpu           = {out_cpu:.4?}");
    println!("  cuda un-fused = {out_nofus:.4?}");
    println!("  cuda fused    = {out_fused:.4?}   <-- wrong for rows > 0");
    println!("  cuda fused/row= {out_single:.4?}");
}
```

Run with `cargo run --release`.

