## Context

ONNX's `Expand` operator uses max-semantics: for each dimension, `output_dim = max(input_dim, shape_dim)`. This means `Expand(tensor_of_shape_[3], shape=[1])` produces output shape `[3]`, not an error.

Burn's `Tensor::expand` follows PyTorch-style semantics where the target shape must be >= input shape in every dimension (or -1 to keep the input dim). Calling `.expand([1])` on a shape-[3] tensor panics:

```
thread panicked at burn-tensor/src/tensor/api/base.rs:3137:
Broadcast arguments must be positive or -1
```

## Current workaround

burn-onnx generates a max-semantics fixup loop before calling Burn's expand ([source](https://github.com/tracel-ai/burn-onnx/blob/main/crates/burn-onnx/src/burn/node/expand.rs#L64-L80)):

```rust
let onnx_shape: [i64; N] = shape;
let input_dims = input.dims();
let mut shape = onnx_shape;
for i in 0..INPUT_RANK {
    let dim_offset = OUTPUT_RANK - INPUT_RANK + i;
    if shape[dim_offset] == 1 && input_dims[i] > 1 {
        shape[dim_offset] = input_dims[i] as i64;
    }
}
input.expand(shape)
```

This works but adds overhead in every generated Expand node.

## Proposal

Add a method (or extend `expand`) that applies max-semantics natively:

```rust
// Option A: new method
tensor.expand_broadcast([1])  // returns shape [3] via max(3, 1)

// Option B: extend expand to treat 1 like -1 when input_dim > 1
tensor.expand([1])  // keeps dim 3 instead of panicking
```

Option B matches ONNX and numpy broadcasting conventions. Option A avoids changing existing behavior.

## References

- [ONNX Expand spec](https://onnx.ai/onnx/operators/onnx__Expand.html): "Broadcast the input tensor following the given shape and the broadcast rule."
- Related: #4506 (Burn API limitations affecting ONNX operator coverage)
- Discovered in burn-onnx [PR #232](https://github.com/tracel-ai/burn-onnx/pull/232) while adding SDXL UNet model check

ONNX import via `burn-onnx` is able to tackle more and more models.
As support grows it unlocks seeing how well these models perform.
In the Nvidia ecosystem TensorRT is generally the fastest inference you get to my knowledge.

